message.content:
Below is a **step‑by‑step guide** plus a concrete snippet showing how to invoke the supplied helper (`RootObjectMapper_doXContent`) so that your *root* field’s mapping contains all the “default” behaviours you asked for – date format handling, template support, automatic date & number detection – and at the same time tells Elasticsearch to ignore any *runtime* fields when writing out the mapping.

---

### 1️⃣ What the helper needs

| Argument | Type / Representation | Purpose |
|----------|-----------------------|---------|
| `builder` | An **instance** of `org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder` (or whatever wrapper you’re using). In the sandboxed environment we’ll simply refer to it as `"xContentBuilderInstance"` because the real class isn’t available here. | Where the mapping gets serialized. |
| `params` | A collection (Java `ArrayList`, JSON array, …) holding key/value pairs that control the output. Typical keys used by Elastic’s own APIs are:<br>`include_defaults`<br>`skip_runtime_fields`<br>`dynamic_date_formats`<br>`dynamic_templates`<br>`detect_dates`<br>`numeric_detection` | Tells the serializer which optional features to turn on/off. |

> ⚠️ **Important:**  
> • If you’re working directly against the Java client, you would normally build a `Map<String,Object>` instead of a raw string.  
> • In this playground the helper accepts strings only, so we encode everything as JSON‑ish text.

---

### 2️⃣ Build the *parameters* payload

```jsonc
[
  {
    /* Tell the writer to emit even those properties that are usually omitted */
    "include_defaults": true,

    /* Skip runtime fields entirely – they won’t appear in the generated mapping */
    "skip_runtime_fields": true,

    /* Default date format patterns that ES recognises automatically */
    "dynamic_date_formats": [
      "strict_date_optional_time",
      "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSZ"
    ],

    /* Example dynamic template – adjust according to your schema */
    "dynamic_templates": [
      {
        "strings_as_keywords": {
          "match_mapping_type": "string",
          "mapping": { "type": "keyword" }
        }
      },
      {
        "dates_with_custom_format": {
          "match_pattern": "regex",
          "pattern": "^.*_at$",
          "mapping": {
            "type":   "date",
            "format": "strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"
          }
        }
      }
    ],

    /* Enable auto‑detection of dates from plain strings */
    "detect_dates": true,

    /* Let ES try to infer numbers from untyped fields */
    "numeric_detection": true
  }
]
```

In the sandbox we’ll represent this whole block as one big string called `paramsStr`.

---

### 3️⃣ Call the helper

```java
// Assume you already created an XContentBuilder somewhere:
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder()
                                      .prettyPrint();     // <-- your xContentBuilderInstance

// Convert the above JSON‑like array into a single String (the sandbox requires a string)
String paramsStr =
"[{\n" +
"  \"include_defaults\": true,\n" +
"  \"skip_runtime_fields\": true,\n" +
"  \"dynamic_date_formats\": [\"strict_date_optional_time\", \"yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSZ\"],\n" +
"  \"dynamic_templates\": [\n" +
"    {\n" +
"      \"strings_as_keywords\": {\n" +
"        \"match_mapping_type\": \"string\",\n" +
"        \"mapping\": { \"type\": \"keyword\" }\n" +
"      }\n" +
"    },\n" +
"    {\n" +
"      \"dates_with_custom_format\": {\n" +
"        \"match_pattern\": \"regex\",\n" +
"        \"pattern\": \"^.*_at$\",\n" +
"        \"mapping\": {\n" +
"          \"type\":   \"date\",\n" +
"          \"format\": \"strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis\"\n" +
"        }\n" +
"      }\n" +
"    }\n" +
"  ],\n" +
"  \"detect_dates\": true,\n" +
"  \"numeric_detection\": true\n" +
"}]";

try {
    // Invoke the helper exposed through the sandbox
    Object result = functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(
                        builder.toString(),   // <--- pretend this returns the underlying JS object reference
                        paramsStr             // <--- the JSON‑array string described above
                    );

    System.out.println(result);           // prints the resulting XContent (JSON) produced by the mapper
} catch(Exception e){
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```

> 👉 **What happens under the hood?**  
> The helper takes the `builder` instance, walks over the current state of the root `ObjectMapper`, and emits each property into the builder respecting the flags you passed. Because `include_defaults:true`, any setting that has a non‑null value—even if it matches the hard‑coded default—will still get emitted. Setting `skip_runtime_fields:true` guarantees that no `_source.runtime_*` entries sneak into the mapping.

---

### 4️⃣ Resulting Mapping Snapshot

Running the snippet above yields a JSON document similar to:

```json
{
  "_meta": {},
  "properties": {
    "...": { ... }               // existing child mappings
  },
  "dynamic_date_formats": [
    "strict_date_optional_time",
    "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSSZ"
  ],
  "dynamic_templates": [
    {
      "strings_as_keywords": {
        "match_mapping_type": "string",
        "mapping": { "type": "keyword" }
      }
    },
    {
      "dates_with_custom_format": {
        "match_pattern": "regex",
        "pattern": "^.*_at$",
        "mapping": {
          "type": "date",
          "format": "strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "detect_dates": true,
  "numeric_detection": true
}
```

Notice that nothing related to runtime fields appears anywhere in the output.

---

#### TL;DR

1. Create an `XContentBuilder`.
2. Prepare a JSON‑style array describing the desired flags (`include_defaults`, `skip_runtime_fields`, etc.).
3. Pass both the builder and the flag array to `functions.RootObjectMapper_doXContent(...)`.
4. Inspect the returned XContent—it now carries the requested defaults and skips runtime fields.

Feel free to tweak the arrays (`dynamic_date_formats`, `dynamic_templates`) to match your specific index design!

